# Character tables, for symmetry analysis of molecular orbitals.
#
# data/client/config/PointGroups already lists the IRREP NAMES of 46
# groups, which is all that was needed to label an orbital.  Deciding
# which irreps a set of orbitals spans needs the characters themselves,
# which is what this file adds.
#
# FORMAT.  One block per group, blank-line separated:
#
#   [GROUP] h=<order>
#   classes: <name> <name> ...        the conjugacy classes, in order
#   counts:  <n>    <n>    ...        how many operations in each
#   <irrep>: <chi>  <chi>  ...        one row per irreducible rep
#
# The irrep names must match PointGroups' spelling for the same group,
# since that is what the codes' own ORBSYM labels are compared against.
#
# The "translations" line records what the Cartesian coordinates x, y, z
# span in that group.  It is there because orthogonality alone CANNOT
# catch two rows being swapped -- a relabelling leaves every orthogonality
# relation intact, so the table stays self-consistent while two irreps
# have each other's names.  The character of the 3-D Cartesian
# representation is computable from the class names alone (1+2cos(theta)
# for a proper rotation, -1+2cos(theta) for an improper one, 1 for a
# mirror, -3 for inversion), so reducing it and comparing against this
# line ties each label to something physical and pins the labelling too.
#
# THESE ARE VERIFIED MECHANICALLY, not by eye.  The great orthogonality
# theorem makes the data self-checking -- rows orthogonal, columns
# orthogonal, sum of counts equal to h, sum of squared dimensions equal
# to h, and every row's own norm equal to h.  tests/symmetry enforces all
# five, so a transcription slip in a hand-entered table cannot survive.
# That is the only reason entering them by hand is acceptable.
#
# COMPLEX CHARACTERS.  Groups with a cyclic part (C3-C6, Cnh, S4/S6/S8,
# T, Th) have irreps that come in complex-conjugate pairs.  Each pair is
# stored as ONE real two-dimensional row with characters
# eps^k + eps^-k = 2cos(2 pi k j/n), which is how the codes label it
# (E, E1, E', E"...).  Such rows are listed on a
#
#   separable: <irrep> <irrep> ...
#
# line.  A separable row has norm 2h, not h, and stands for two 1-D
# irreps, so the checks count it twice (as many irreps as classes,
# squared dimensions) and CharacterTable::reduce() divides its raw sum
# by 2h, giving the number of copies of the real E.  Their characters
# are irrational in general (2cos72 ...), so rounding is not allowed.
#
# Characters are from standard tables, verified mechanically.

